#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868" :question/problem "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870" :question/problem "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](images/d5/d526e46960ff276b3249961ad4939cb2ac2f84fd2dba97644df92b96972dc36e.svg)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](images/83/8387c02793e59a20c3c126da147ca4b1f6812e11f0173eed6e1768be85a07f7d.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c388af8-c41d-5c6d-8140-460cd30fd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c388af8-c41d-5c6d-8140-460cd30fd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365894} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a42bbe3-769a-5a38-9e0d-7251654af238" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed9527e-50d0-53be-a3d3-45d4754a6c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-342712" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(4)?$\n\n$W(4)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccbbbe03-535c-5a98-8643-6d9012b867e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216669} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb0a8b5-e74e-55d8-9b13-0e8abbe8a521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216671} {:answer/id #uuid "1e804472-aa9d-546b-8e8f-780a182772a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496721} {:answer/id #uuid "dc69df00-8902-553c-9a7e-a1f2b9bc5e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{q(x)}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eb0a8b5-e74e-55d8-9b13-0e8abbe8a521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216671} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1285df3-060a-5fc6-be97-37ff5f0e6f8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216668} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496973} {:answer/id #uuid "cc020a0a-3e91-54e9-b3ff-3a8b6b0c9421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497077} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1a3557-e8a9-5474-820f-a54d260979a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496973} :answer-field/id #uuid "1458f4ab-9e6b-58ec-a4d4-41759c631919" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d9e74e1-73cc-5d9b-92c4-713de893186a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496863} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496983} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc27853-144c-5d1c-822c-2cb725c6bfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497129} {:answer/id #uuid "e249b4b4-00d4-5097-8015-f90a8f3174d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496983} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a05a66-bb84-597b-8204-53d4da9cb137" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72291c8c-7d08-5518-8b04-62e5d8790c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342713" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa976e1-3e6f-58f6-82a7-be56b4d82d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496689} {:answer/id #uuid "27c0ac50-87b6-504b-8aa2-8e7d4b4a7825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496742} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc4c3e0-a11f-5140-bce9-cea64f5d6ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496796} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef6055-4626-5c9e-bf8c-d479b5bed105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496902} {:answer/id #uuid "856e8138-9b56-52ff-9727-64cdd4f40c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa976e1-3e6f-58f6-82a7-be56b4d82d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496689} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e650917-9c8c-5399-8075-d5c120d8aecb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64a84975-134c-59e7-b3de-faa7ec811813" :question/math-academy-id "q-343254" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4){y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =(4-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d77649-575e-537e-8070-76776b57eae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496751} {:answer/id #uuid "47d77792-7b25-57c9-bdc6-bc189048b501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496817} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae419f-7598-55b0-a2a6-b537e443ae0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})-1)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496987} {:answer/id #uuid "db677ac0-7b00-5ae1-8c79-1afd9dadd6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497122} {:answer/id #uuid "e61bd4ff-6144-5a7e-8e02-00c3e609c979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61bd4ff-6144-5a7e-8e02-00c3e609c979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497148} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4ae727-1afb-5ef9-87af-f452ca62c1df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2c79c80-99de-54b9-9d82-c2f07cd702f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})-1){y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))W(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033f2fdc-fe43-5a14-be7f-944d2c6065ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496662} {:answer/id #uuid "0342a05b-3acf-5c2d-a620-4ebb95fdb8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496663} {:answer/id #uuid "12100e57-b84a-5582-a156-8cd0000a882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496696} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb2fca6-0bdc-5251-bb05-7a59b21a0c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496719} {:answer/id #uuid "c11b89d5-c585-5b5a-b4e4-4aab378e5779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0342a05b-3acf-5c2d-a620-4ebb95fdb8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496663} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f054df8-5528-5182-a3c2-38d97e30f9c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "753b45d7-da51-5748-9817-073e29597daf" :question/math-academy-id "q-343261" :question/problem "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x+1$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+1}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{x+1}W(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fd6574-74c6-5472-8ec2-afc12139c3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496660} {:answer/id #uuid "44c5cc9a-98a5-502e-a69e-dbbe117ba10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496805} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bf9e57-035f-5b67-9c47-089330d6eab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497033} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bd9306-b505-5e86-aab8-b589a392dc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3xW(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497062} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bbf0ae-9b58-5f81-baee-891729f34ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fd6574-74c6-5472-8ec2-afc12139c3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496660} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae4a21d-846d-5371-89fc-e2dd8e9af3e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32e7120e-ecd0-54a1-8649-0fea9e5b153d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343262" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by ${x}^{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e9f80a-809c-565a-8d3e-f30a614ba078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496752} {:answer/id #uuid "57522148-92ca-5be5-9f64-7d028cd0d2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{2}{x})W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496844} {:answer/id #uuid "65a0e4ce-bd6e-5296-8a0a-5fd18bf74b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496879} {:answer/id #uuid "6df2da19-269f-5bba-a866-2d28fc031ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496896} {:answer/id #uuid "b15944cc-b6d7-5607-bb8a-096b5d8506b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4})W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15944cc-b6d7-5607-bb8a-096b5d8506b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4})W(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497020} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dfaec97-3c0a-5f5b-9b6f-70e8a9d7277c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25dddbef-ad84-5535-a187-268eebd173d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343263" :question/problem "$$\n4x{y}^{″}+({x}^{2}-8){y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?\n\n$\\,$\n${W}^{′}(x)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $4x$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{4x}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4})W(x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe95508-90a4-5739-800e-6730fc486775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316341} {:answer/id #uuid "0d71e84b-d618-5a17-93c9-4e4987903896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d71e84b-d618-5a17-93c9-4e4987903896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496682} :answer-field/id #uuid "de9f196c-0661-5c94-9e3f-43c39b8d82e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "283b979f-8c0c-5af7-8c3b-c602adbe88b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343272" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0157b7bd-12dd-5467-9e0b-69e6b3b508f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0157b7bd-12dd-5467-9e0b-69e6b3b508f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{-12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496661} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ed920b-2433-56d0-bfb7-61417eb6d9c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53cebc23-dff1-56fe-a57a-9a0d9ca25a96" :question/math-academy-id "q-343274" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(2)=5.$ What is $W(6)?$\n\n$W(6)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-6} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(6) & =5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3\\cdot 6}=5{e}^{-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95dea895-ae65-5d43-951c-870afd31fe30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95dea895-ae65-5d43-951c-870afd31fe30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315949} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a34654-f2e5-50e3-ab8b-574824d4527e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04fad153-de50-519c-b7c7-6082be26f701" :question/math-academy-id "q-343281" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=1.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b13469-f244-5596-aa8e-0a73e70120fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85b13469-f244-5596-aa8e-0a73e70120fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496934} :answer-field/id #uuid "d647e5a2-359a-5de6-8d63-fc0680cdce87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa87e239-f514-5ab3-af2e-43a62163086f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343283" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=10.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}({x}^{2}+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=10$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =\\frac{C}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\ 10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{10}{{2}^{2}+1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138c7775-7211-5916-a88f-f34085f4e58d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496702} {:answer/id #uuid "16dd2faf-97cd-5227-a69c-a24ad399df7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496707} {:answer/id #uuid "47985eb4-2da3-590b-9c9f-debdadc43135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496816} {:answer/id #uuid "d43c47fe-8dfe-5ab2-bf30-ae9c8091017a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16dd2faf-97cd-5227-a69c-a24ad399df7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496707} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d6975b2-cd8f-53c8-a76b-cbe26c306432" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245b91f9-9a34-52df-a77c-4058508e007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496736} {:answer/id #uuid "474d5070-8a51-5457-abf9-ed6cc2756bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496815} {:answer/id #u