26-f055c20928ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-343290" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=5.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=5,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-1W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=5{e}^{2}{e}^{-x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7fb997a-9a15-5f71-8d90-1aff6caf0292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$